### How are sealed types and records two halves of one design?

A sealed hierarchy is a sum type: the value is exactly one of a closed set of alternatives. A record is a product type: the value bundles named components. Together they form algebraic data types, and Java's pattern matching consumes both in one step.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public sealed interface Txn
        permits Txn.Deposit, Txn.Withdrawal, Txn.Transfer {

    record Deposit(String account, BigDecimal amount) implements Txn {}
    record Withdrawal(String account, BigDecimal amount) implements Txn {}
    record Transfer(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) implements Txn {}
}
```

### How do record patterns deconstruct in a switch?

A record pattern binds the record's components directly in the case, so you never call accessor methods.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.Txn;
import com.bank.model.Txn.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class TxnProcessor {

    public String describe(Txn txn) {
        return switch (txn) {
            case Deposit(var account, var amount) ->
                    "Deposit " + amount + " INR to " + account;
            case Withdrawal(var account, var amount) ->
                    "Withdraw " + amount + " INR from " + account;
            case Transfer(var from, var to, var amount) ->
                    "Transfer " + amount + " INR: " + from + " -> " + to;
        };
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TxnProcessor processor = new TxnProcessor();
        System.out.println(processor.describe(
                new Deposit("SAV-1001", new BigDecimal("50000"))));
        System.out.println(processor.describe(
                new Transfer("SAV-1001", "SAV-2002", new BigDecimal("25000"))));
    }
}
```

```text
Deposit 50000 INR to SAV-1001
Transfer 25000 INR: SAV-1001 -> SAV-2002
```

### How do guarded patterns add business logic?

Guards refine a case with a boolean condition using the `when` keyword, so you branch not just on shape but on values.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.Txn;
import com.bank.model.Txn.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class ComplianceProcessor {
    private static final BigDecimal LIMIT = new BigDecimal("100000");

    public String classify(Txn txn) {
        return switch (txn) {
            case Transfer(var f, var t, var amount)
                    when amount.compareTo(LIMIT) > 0 ->
                    "HIGH VALUE transfer: " + amount + " INR (needs approval)";
            case Transfer(var f, var t, var amount) ->
                    "Normal transfer: " + amount + " INR";
            case Deposit(var account, var amount) ->
                    "Deposit: " + amount + " INR";
            case Withdrawal(var account, var amount) ->
                    "Withdrawal: " + amount + " INR";
        };
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ComplianceProcessor proc = new ComplianceProcessor();
        System.out.println(proc.classify(
                new Transfer("SAV-1001", "SAV-2002", new BigDecimal("250000"))));
        System.out.println(proc.classify(
                new Transfer("SAV-1001", "SAV-2002", new BigDecimal("5000"))));
    }
}
```

```text
HIGH VALUE transfer: 250000 INR (needs approval)
Normal transfer: 5000 INR
```

### How do nested record patterns work?

When a record holds another record, you can destructure both in a single pattern.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public sealed interface Txn2 {
    record AccountInfo(String id, BigDecimal balance) {}
    record Transfer(AccountInfo from, AccountInfo to, BigDecimal amount)
            implements Txn2 {}
    record Deposit(AccountInfo account, BigDecimal amount)
            implements Txn2 {}
}
```

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.Txn2;
import com.bank.model.Txn2.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class NestedPatternDemo {
    public String process(Txn2 txn) {
        return switch (txn) {
            // Nested deconstruction: pull fields from inner record
            case Transfer(AccountInfo(var fromId, var fromBal),
                          AccountInfo(var toId, var toBal), var amount) ->
                    fromId + " (bal: " + fromBal + ") -> "
                            + toId + " (bal: " + toBal + "): " + amount + " INR";
            case Deposit(AccountInfo(var id, var bal), var amount) ->
                    "Deposit " + amount + " to " + id + " (bal: " + bal + ")";
        };
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        NestedPatternDemo demo = new NestedPatternDemo();
        System.out.println(demo.process(new Transfer(
                new AccountInfo("SAV-1001", new BigDecimal("500000")),
                new AccountInfo("SAV-2002", new BigDecimal("200000")),
                new BigDecimal("75000")
        )));
    }
}
```

```text
SAV-1001 (bal: 500000) -> SAV-2002 (bal: 200000): 75000 INR
```

### Why are records implicitly final?

Records are implicitly `final`, so they satisfy the sealed-subtype rule automatically. When a sealed interface permits a record, you do not need to write `final` on the record.

```java
// Records are implicitly final: this just works
sealed interface PaymentResult {
    record Success(BigDecimal amount) implements PaymentResult {}
    record Failure(String reason) implements PaymentResult {}
}
// No 'final' keyword needed on Success or Failure
```

### What happens when you add a new record type?

Every exhaustive switch that does not handle the new type fails to compile. This turns "forgot a case" from a runtime bug into a compile error.

```java
// Add a new permitted type:
// record Pending(String expectedDate) implements PaymentResult {}

// Every existing switch over PaymentResult now fails to compile:
// error: the switch expression does not cover all possible input values
```

### How is this different from the old instanceof chain?

| Feature | `instanceof` chain | Sealed + Record pattern switch |
|---|---|---|
| Exhaustiveness | Not checked by compiler | Compiler-verified |
| Deconstruction | Manual cast + getters | Direct component binding |
| Adding a variant | Silently missed | Compile error |
| Null handling | Manual NPE risk | Explicit `case null` |

### What should a senior engineer know?

Prefer exhaustive switches without a `default` over a sealed type, because a `default` would silently swallow a newly added subtype and defeat the exhaustiveness check. Records as data shapes plus sealed interfaces as sum types is the idiomatic Java 21+ pattern for domain modelling. Guards with `when` layer business rules without breaking exhaustiveness.
